>
  <h2 id="what-is-dna">What is DNA?</h2>

  <p>
    DNA is a description language for business systems. You describe the organization in JSON, and from that description
    you can generate documentation, workflows, validation, and, increasingly, working software.
  </p>

  <p>
    The layer documented today is <strong>Operational DNA</strong>: what the organization is, and what it does. It is
    written in two vocabularies that stay apart, so structure can be modelled first and given behaviour later.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/docs/operational#structure-vocabulary">Structure</a></strong> (<code>structure/v1</code>):
      domains, entities, attributes, and relationships.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/docs/operational#behaviour-vocabulary">Behaviour</a></strong> (<code>behaviour/v1</code>):
      actions, operations, workflows, conditions, and policies, each naming the structure domain it describes.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Product</strong> (what gets built) and <strong>Technology</strong> (how it is bound and deployed) are the layers
    that follow. Their reference is not published yet.
    <a href="/docs#coming-soon">Coming soon</a> on the docs landing names them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quickstart">Quickstart: a structure document and its behaviour</h2>

  <p>
    A structure document says what exists. This one is a lending vocabulary with a single entity, an application, whose
    <code>status</code> is a state attribute. Movement between those states is not structure. It is behaviour.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "dna": "structure/v1",
  "id": "lending",
  "name": "Lending",
  "description": "A loan application and where it stands.",
  "entities": [
    {
      "id": "application",
      "name": "Application",
      "description": "A request to borrow.",
      "attributes": [
        {
          "name": "status",
          "type": "state",
          "description": "Where the application is. What moves it is behaviour.",
          "states": ["received", "funded", "declined"],
          "initial": "received"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    A behaviour document names that domain and attaches an action. <code>fund</code> is a capability of
    <code>application</code>. Its transition moves <code>status</code> from <code>received</code> to <code>funded</code
    >.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "dna": "behaviour/v1",
  "id": "lending-behaviour",
  "name": "Lending behaviour",
  "description": "How an application is decided.",
  "domain": "lending",
  "actions": [
    {
      "id": "fund",
      "entity": "application",
      "name": "Fund",
      "description": "Fund an application that is clear to close.",
      "transition": { "from": ["received"], "to": "funded" }
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The <a href="/docs/operational">Operational reference</a> is generated from the schemas those documents have to match.
    The <a href="/docs/examples">examples</a> are the reference domains (checkout, origination, diligence, agent registration)
    the schemas are held to.
  </p>

